BM25 (Best Matching 25) is a classic text-ranking algorithm used by search engines to estimate the relevance of a document to a given 
user query. It is the gold standard for keyword-based search (sparse retrieval).Instead of understanding the deep meaning of words 
like a vector embedding does, BM25 looks at exact keyword matches using ,

two core smart concepts:

1. The Core Mechanics
 - Term Frequency (TF) with Saturation: It counts how many times a query word appears in a document. However, it applies a "saturation" 
   limit. Seeing a keyword 2 times is much better than 1 time, but seeing it 20 times is not 10 times better than seeing it 2 times.
 - Inverse Document Frequency (IDF): It penalizes common words. If your query is "the database design", BM25 gives very little weight to 
   "the", medium weight to "database", and high weight to "design" because "design" is rarer across the whole dataset
 - Document Length Normalization: It penalizes extremely long documents. A short document containing your keyword 3 times is considered 
   much more relevant than a massive 100-page document containing your keyword 3 times.

🔄 BM25 vs. Vector Embeddings (Ollama/BGE)
In modern learning architectures, BM25 and Vector Search are often used together because they have opposite, complementary strengths:

Feature             | BM25 (Keyword Search)                | Vector Search (Semantic Search)      |
--------------------|--------------------------------------|--------------------------------------|
How it finds data   | Exact keyword matching               | Conceptual/Meaning matching          |
Handles typos?Poorly| (needs exact letters)                | Excellent (understands context)      |
Handles jargon/IDs? | Perfectly (finds exact part numbers) | Poorly (obscure IDs look like noise) |
Computational cost  | Extremely cheap and fast             |Heavy (requires GP                    |


BM25 finds text that contains the important words from the query, and scores how well each document/chunk matches those words.

